EXAMPLE 5 Solve the system of equations 4x + \( \frac{6}{y} \) = 15 and 6x - \( \frac{8}{y} \) = 14 and hence find 'p' if y = px - 2

SOLUTION The given system of equation is

4x + \( \frac{6}{y} \) = 15 \hspace{1cm} \text{... (i)}

6x - \( \frac{8}{y} \) = 14 \hspace{1cm} \text{... (ii)}

Multiplying equation (i) by 4 and equation (ii) by 3, we obtain

16x + \( \frac{24}{y} \) = 60 \hspace{1cm} \text{... (iii)}

18x - \( \frac{24}{y} \) = 42 \hspace{1cm} \text{... (iv)}

Adding (iv) and (iii), we get: 34x = 102 ⇒ x = 3.

Putting x = 3 in equation (i), we get: 12 + \( \frac{6}{y} \) = 15 ⇒ y = 2

Hence, the solution of the given system of equations is x = 3, y = 2.

Putting x = 3, y = 2 in y = px - 2, we get: 2 = 3p - 2 ⇒ p = 4/3

BASED ON LOWER ORDER THINKING SKILLS (LOTS)

EXAMPLE 6 Solve: \( \frac{1}{2(2x + 3y)} \) + \( \frac{12}{7(3x - 2y)} \) = \( \frac{1}{2} \)

\( \frac{7}{2x + 3y} \) + \( \frac{4}{3x - 2y} \) = 2, where 2x + 3y ≠ 0 and 3x - 2y ≠ 0.

SOLUTION Let \( \frac{1}{2x + 3y} \) = u and \( \frac{1}{3x - 2y} \) = v. Then, the given system of equations becomes

\( \frac{1}{2} \)u + \( \frac{12}{7} \)v = \( \frac{1}{2} \) ⇒ 7u + 24v = 7 \hspace{1cm} \text{... (i)}

and,

7u + 4v = 2 \hspace{1cm} \text{... (ii)}

Subtracting equation (ii) from equation (i), we get: 20v = 5 ⇒ v = \( \frac{1}{4} \)

Putting v = \( \frac{1}{4} \) in equation (i), we get: 7u + 6 = 7 ⇒ u = \( \frac{1}{7} \).

Now, u = \( \frac{1}{7} \) ⇒ \( \frac{1}{2x + 3y} \) = \( \frac{1}{7} \) ⇒ 2x + 3y = 7 \hspace{1cm} \text{... (iii)}

and, v = \( \frac{1}{4} \) ⇒ \( \frac{1}{3x - 2y} \) = \( \frac{1}{4} \) ⇒ 3x - 2y = 4 \hspace{1cm} \text{... (iv)}

Multiplying equation (iii) by 2 and equation (iv) by 3, we get

4x + 6y = 14 \hspace{1cm} \text{... (v)}

9x - 6y = 12 \hspace{1cm} \text{... (vi)}

Adding equations (v) and (vi), we get: 13x = 26 ⇒ x = 2

Putting x = 2 in equation (v), we get: 8 + 6y = 14 ⇒ y = 1
Hence, x = 2, y = 1 is the solution of the given system of equations.

EXAMPLE 7 Solve: \( \frac{5}{x+y} - \frac{2}{x-y} = -1 \)
\( \frac{15}{x+y} + \frac{7}{x-y} = 10 \), where x + y ≠ 0 and x - y ≠ 0.

SOLUTION Let \( u = \frac{1}{x+y} \) and \( v = \frac{1}{x-y} \). Then, the given system of equations becomes
5u - 2v = -1 \quad \text{...(i)}
15u + 7v = 10 \quad \text{...(ii)}
Multiplying equation (i) by 3, this system of equations becomes
15u - 6v = -3 \quad \text{...(iii)}
15u + 7v = 10 \quad \text{...(iv)}
Subtracting equation (iv) from (iii), we get: -13v = -13 ⇒ v = 1. Putting v = 1 in equation (i), we get
5u - 2 = -1 ⇒ u = \frac{1}{5}
Now, \( u = \frac{1}{5} ⇒ \frac{1}{x+y} = \frac{1}{5} ⇒ x+y = 5 \) \quad \text{...(v)}
and, \( v = 1 ⇒ \frac{1}{x-y} = 1 ⇒ x-y = 1 \) \quad \text{...(vi)}
Adding (vi) and (v), we get: 2x = 6 ⇒ x = 3. Putting x = 3 in equation (v), we get: y = 2.
Hence, x = 3, y = 2 is the solution of the given system of equations.

EXAMPLE 8 Solve: 3(2u + v) = 7uv
3(u + 3v) = 11uv

SOLUTION Clearly, the given equations are not linear equations in the variables u and v but can be reduced to linear equations by an appropriate substitution.
If we put u = 0 in either of the two equations, we get v = 0. So, u = 0, v = 0 form a solution of the given system of equations. To find the other solutions, we assume that u ≠ 0, v ≠ 0.
Now, u ≠ 0, v ≠ 0 ⇒ uv ≠ 0.
On dividing each one of the given equations by uv, we get
\( \frac{6}{v} + \frac{2}{u} = 7 \) \quad \text{...(i)}
\( \frac{3}{v} + \frac{9}{u} = 11 \) \quad \text{...(ii)}
